     <narrative xml:lang="EN">WSH-I4 - Number of people having access to sufficient and safe water for domestic use</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rehabilitation , repair and flushing of 8 boreholes. 1440 benificiaries will be able to collect 15 l pp/pd =total 12.960</narrative>
    </description>
    <reference code="WSH-I4" indicator-uri="https://www.zoa-international.com" vocabulary="99"></reference>
    <baseline value="0" year="2024"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-09-23"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-12-31"></period-end>
     <actual value="0">
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">0 out of 8 boreholes yet to be rehabilitated. However, all the 8 boreholes are assessed and BoQ completed</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2025-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-02-20"></period-end>
     <target value="9.890"></target>
     <actual value="29253">
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">The action identified and repaired 9 Solar powered Boreholes damaged by the flood in Jere and MMC. Each Borehole has an average yield of  1.5l/s or 43,200ltrs in 8hrs of daily pumping per boreholes. This is equivalent to 2880 persons per day for each borehole. Total number of individuals having access to safe and sufficient water supply through solar powered  borehole rehabilitation is: 2880x9 = 25,920 individuals In Gubio Camp, ZOA set--up a water trucking point for immediate supply of water. Five (5) 5000 litres of tank was setup to receive and distribute water for the IDPs. This amounts to a daily capacity of 50,000 litres per day for 60 days serving 3,333 individuals in the camp setting. The total water supply capacity supported by this response = 25,920 + 3,333 = 29,253</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
